{"qid":"Q1179","names":["Aquitaine"],"title":"Aquitaine","endonym":{"native":"Aquitaine","lang":"fr"},"capsule":"Aquitaine was a territory of southwestern Gaul — roughly modern Bordeaux, Gascony, and the Garonne basin — that passed from Roman provincia to Visigothic control in the fifth century, then to Frankish rule after Clovis's defeat of the Visigoths at Vouillé in 507. Charlemagne organized it as a sub-kingdom for his son Louis (later Louis the Pious) in 781, and it was subsequently contested between Carolingian successor kingdoms; by the mid-ninth century it operated as a semi-autonomous duchy under the Guilhemid and later Ramnulfid counts. The dataset span to 895 reflects the period before Aquitaine consolidated as a stable Capetian dependency, with the duchy fragmenting across competing noble houses during the Viking incursions of the late ninth century.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Carolingian Aquitaine's boundaries are moderately attested from royal charters and ecclesiastical documents, but precise sub-county frontier delineation in the dataset is interpolated and approximate.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Aquitaine","url":"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Aquitaine"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Aquitaine","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aquitaine"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Aquitaine","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Chram","nativeName":"Chramnus","roman":"Chram","start":555,"end":560,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Bladast","nativeName":"Bladastes","roman":"Bladast","start":585,"end":587,"image":null,"imageUrl":null